--TEST--
`new` over a `class<T>` is refused when an implementor of `T` declares a constructor that cannot take the arguments written (`rule:classes/constructor-compatibility`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A class reference may hold any implementor of its bound, so a `new` through
// one has to be a call every implementor accepts. `Strict` demands a second
// argument, and no `new $cls(...)` written against `Formatter`'s constructor
// can supply it — which PHP discovers at run time and Novis refuses at the
// `new` site, where the arguments are.
class Formatter {
    public function constructor(public string $prefix) {}
}

class Strict extends Formatter {
    public function constructor(string $prefix, public int $width) {
        parent::constructor($prefix);
    }
}

// Refused where it is written and not at `Strict`'s declaration: a subclass
// nothing instantiates through a class reference is nobody's problem, and the
// fix is local to this line — `as class<Strict>` and instantiate that.
class<Formatter> $cls = Formatter::class as class<Formatter>;
Formatter $made = new $cls("p: ");
echo $made->prefix, "\n";
// covers: lang:classes/inheritance
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0794]: `new` over `class<Formatter>` cannot check its arguments
   --> %s:21:19
   |
21 | Formatter $made = new $cls("p: ");
   |                   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ `Strict::constructor` is not compatible with `Formatter::constructor`
   = help: a class reference may hold any implementor, so every one of them has to accept the arguments written here — give `Strict` a constructor compatible with `Formatter`'s, or narrow this to `as class<Strict>` and instantiate that

error: aborting due to 1 error
